{"abstract":"This paper proposes a structured methodology to evaluate AI-generated game narratives, leveraging the Delphi study structure with a panel of narrative design experts. Our approach synthesizes story quality dimensions from literature and expert insights, mapping them into the Kano model framework to understand their impact on player satisfaction. The results can inform game developers on prioritizing quality aspects when co-creating game narratives with generative AI.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Valdivia Martinez, Arturo","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828162"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Delphi method","Kano model","Player satisfaction"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evaluating quality of gaming narratives co created with ai","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.04239","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828162","ids":["itu-81e56ec3-7811-444c-aab5-7826d766c041"],"keyword":["AI-generated narratives","Delphi method","Kano model","game narrative quality","player satisfaction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A8D795706950FE7F74BA59B3ACF9E18D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.04239","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Arturo","key":"valdiviamartinezpau","last":"Valdivia Martinez","locid":"06b69b03-fa50-410d-a8e5-fd3da1876353","name":"Valdivia Martinez, Arturo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04239","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2025 IEEE Conference on Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-81e56ec3-7811-444c-aab5-7826d766c041"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evaluating Quality of Gaming Narratives Co-created with AI","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828162","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Cheating in online video games compromises the integrity of gaming experiences. Anti-cheat systems, such as VAC (Valve Anti-Cheat), face significant challenges in keeping pace with evolving cheating methods without imposing invasive measures on users' systems. This paper presents AntiCheatPT_256, a transformer-based machine learning model designed to detect cheating behaviour in Counter-Strike 2 using gameplay data. To support this, we introduce and publicly release CS2CD: A labelled dataset of 795 matches. Using this dataset, 90,707 context windows were created and subsequently augmented to address class imbalance. The transformer model, trained on these windows, achieved an accuracy of 89.17% and an AUC of 93.36% on an unaugmented test set. This approach emphasizes reproducibility and real-world applicability, offering a robust baseline for future research in data-driven cheat detection.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Loo, Mille Mei Zhen","Lužkov, Gert","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828150"],"event":{"acronym":"CIG","title":"IEEE Conference on Computational Intelligence and Games"},"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Gameplay data","Machine learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"anticheatpt a transformer based approach to cheat detection in competitive computer games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.06348","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828150","ids":["itu-53677602-6db8-48af-b52d-9b370e77c11d"],"keyword":["Cheat detection","Counter-Strike 2","Gameplay data","Machine learning","Transformer-based model"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-1C58243F4CDEA7C512FC0A9DC5323AA5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2508.06348","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab","IT University of Copenhagen, The Maritime Hub"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mille Mei Zhen","key":"loopau","last":"Loo","name":"Loo, Mille Mei Zhen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gert","key":"lužkovpau","last":"Lužkov","name":"Lužkov, Gert","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2508.06348","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2025 IEEE Conference on Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-53677602-6db8-48af-b52d-9b370e77c11d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"AntiCheatPT: A Transformer-Based Approach to Cheat Detection in Competitive Computer Games","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828150","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Facial mimicry—the automatic, unconscious imitation of others' expressions—is vital for emotional understanding. This study investigates how mimicry differs across emotions using Face Action Units from videos and participants' responses. Dynamic Time Warping quantified the temporal alignment between participants' and stimuli's facial expressions, revealing significant emotional variations. Post-hoc tests indicated greater mimicry for 'Fear' than 'Happy' and reduced mimicry for 'Anger' compared to 'Fear'. The mimicry correlations with personality traits like Extraversion and Agreeableness were significant, showcasing subtle yet meaningful connections. These findings suggest specific emotions evoke stronger mimicry, with personality traits playing a secondary role in emotional alignment. Notably, our results highlight how personality-linked mimicry mechanisms extend beyond interpersonal communication to affective computing applications, such as remote human-human interactions and human-virtual-agent scenarios. Insights from temporal facial mimicry—e.g., designing digital agents that adaptively mirror user expressions—enable developers to create empathetic, personalized systems, enhancing emotional resonance and user engagement.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Seikavandi, Meisam Jamshidi","Fimland, Jostein","Barrett, Maria Jung","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828172"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective Computing","Dynamic Time Warping","Facial mimicry","Personality traits"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exploring the temporal dynamics of facial mimicry in emotion processing using action units","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.17306","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828172","ids":["itu-acb09860-e19d-472a-9d1a-6764e02efa5c"],"keyword":["Affective computing","Dynamic Time Warping","Facial Action Units","Facial mimicry","Personality traits"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-8112E3C9BE96D0A3D9611C78A3F2E46B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.17306","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Meisam Jamshidi","key":"seikavandipau","last":"Seikavandi","locid":"0080607f-9f26-4f73-a974-36b4940fb777","name":"Seikavandi, Meisam Jamshidi","orcid":"0000-0002-1271-2481","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jostein","key":"fimlandpau","last":"Fimland","name":"Fimland, Jostein","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Maria Jung","key":"barrettpau","last":"Barrett","name":"Barrett, Maria Jung","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2503.17306","publisher":"IEEE","series":"IEEE International Conference on Automatic Face and Gesture","status":"p","title":"2025 IEEE International Conference on Automatic Face and Gesture"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-acb09860-e19d-472a-9d1a-6764e02efa5c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Exploring the Temporal Dynamics of Facial Mimicry in Emotion Processing Using Action Units","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828172","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This study investigates the psychophysiological effects of loot box interactions in video games and their potential similarities to gambling-related behaviours. Using electrodermal activity (EDA) measurements, the research examines player arousal during loot box interactions and explores whether individuals with higher Internet Gaming Disorder (IGD) severity exhibit reduced sensitivity to these random reward mechanisms. The study employs a custom-designed game, ``A Minute Outside,'' to control experimental conditions and standardise loot box interactions. Participants' IGD severity is assessed using the Internet Gaming Disorder Scale – Short Form (IGDS9-SF), while arousal is measured through EDA, analysing skin conductance responses (SCRs), area under the curve (AUC), and skin conductance level (SCL). By leveraging psychophysiological methods commonly used in gambling research, this investigation aims to provide objective evidence on whether loot boxes function as gambling-like mechanisms at a physiological level. The study contributes to the ongoing debate surrounding gaming disorder and loot box harms, offering insights for game developers and policymakers on the potential risks associated with these monetisation strategies.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Tedeschi, Gianmarco","Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828182"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Electrodermal activity","Internet gaming disorder","Loot boxes","Psychophysiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"using psychophysiological insights to evaluate the impact of loot boxes on arousal","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.04906","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828182","ids":["itu-dcb7e191-405f-4067-8875-0bbd83af7cd9"],"keyword":["Electrodermal activity","Gambling-like mechanisms","Internet Gaming Disorder","Loot boxes","Psychophysiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-C0776280E6B7DB38B80B3C62FCC90E26"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.04906","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Game Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Games Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gianmarco","key":"tedeschipau","last":"Tedeschi","name":"Tedeschi, Gianmarco","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rune Kristian Lundedal","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"80784458-06f6-4eff-b0c8-df982ffa9835","name":"Nielsen, Rune Kristian Lundedal","orcid":"0000-0002-7209-9041","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2507.04906","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2025 IEEE Conference on Games","volume":"abs/2507.04906"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-dcb7e191-405f-4067-8875-0bbd83af7cd9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Using Psychophysiological Insights to Evaluate the Impact of Loot Boxes on Arousal","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828182","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This paper investigates the challenges of affect control in large language models (LLMs), focusing on their ability to express appropriate emotional states during extended dialogues. We evaluated state-of-the-art open-weight LLMs to assess their affective expressive range in terms of arousal and valence. Our study employs a novel methodology combining LLM-based sentiment analysis with multiturn dialogue simulations between LLMs. We quantify the models' capacity to express a wide spectrum of emotions and how they fluctuate during interactions. Our findings reveal significant variations among LLMs in their ability to maintain consistent affect, with some models demonstrating more stable emotional trajectories than others. Furthermore, we identify key challenges in affect control, including difficulties in producing and maintaining extreme emotional states and limitations in adapting affect to changing conversational contexts. These findings have important implications for the development of more emotionally intelligent AI systems and highlight the need for improved affect modelling in LLMs.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Fazzi, Gino Franco","Skoven Hinge, Julie","Heinrich, Stefan","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798546"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affective Computing","Conversational agents","Emotion recognition","Human Computer Interaction HCI","Large Language Models LLMs","Natural Language Processing NLP"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"don t get too excited eliciting emotions in llms","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.02457","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798546","identifier":[{"id":"105005739891","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-8979b659-ccbc-41f4-9027-394f56a3a013"],"keyword":["Affective computing","Conversational agents","Emotion in AI","Emotion recognition","Emottionally intelligent agents","Human-Computer interaction (HCI)","Large Language Models (LLMs)","Natural language processing (NLP)","Trust and engagement in AI","Valence-arousal space"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-FCC06BA1955CB1612C0235F04BB17BFA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2503.02457","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Gino Franco","key":"fazzipau","last":"Fazzi","name":"Fazzi, Gino Franco","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Julie","key":"skovenhingepau","last":"Skoven Hinge","name":"Skoven Hinge, Julie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2503.02457","eisbn":["{9798400713958/2025/04"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8979b659-ccbc-41f4-9027-394f56a3a013"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Don’t Get Too Excited - Eliciting Emotions in LLMs","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798546","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Video game designers often view confusion as undesirable, yet it is inevitable, as new players must adapt to new interfaces and mechanics in an increasingly varied and innovative game market, which is more popular than ever. Research suggests that confusion can contribute to a positive experience, potentially motivating players to learn. The state of confusion in video games should be further investigated to gain more insight into the learning experience of play and how it affects the player experience. In this article, we design a study to collect learning-related affects for users playing a game prototype that intentionally confuses the player. We assess the gathered affects against a complex learning model, affirming that, in specific instances, the player experience aligns with the learning experiences. Moreover, we identify correlations between these affects and the Player Experience Inventory constructs, particularly concerning flow experiences.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","Jarma Montoya, Oleg","Scirea, Marco","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828170"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Affect","Confusion","FLOW","Learning","Player Experience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the dynamics of affect during play and the role of confusion","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/112428043/CoG_2025_Short_On_the_dynamics_of_affective_states_during_play_and_the_role_of_confusion_4_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828170","ids":["itu-9ce701a3-057e-4657-a53b-4acc1f6f9cac"],"keyword":["affect","confusion","flow","learning","player experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-D808702DF98233A619ACCA0DCA4DB64C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/112428043/CoG_2025_Short_On_the_dynamics_of_affective_states_during_play_and_the_role_of_confusion_4_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["University of Southern Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas Vase Schultz","key":"voldenpau","last":"Volden","locid":"7e884581-3d40-42e6-af12-453e052c9c87","name":"Volden, Thomas Vase Schultz","orcid":"0009-0009-2177-5482","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Oleg","key":"jarmamontoyapau","last":"Jarma Montoya","name":"Jarma Montoya, Oleg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marco","key":"scireapau","last":"Scirea","name":"Scirea, Marco","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/cog64752.2025.11114086","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2025 IEEE Conference on Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-9ce701a3-057e-4657-a53b-4acc1f6f9cac"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"On the Dynamics of Affect During Play and the Role of Confusion","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828170","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Neural decoding is an important method in cognitive neuroscience that aims to decode brain representations from recorded neural activity using a multivariate machine learning model. The THINGS initiative provides a large EEG dataset of 46 subjects watching rapidly shown images. Here, we test the feasibility of using this method for decoding high-level object features using recent deep learning models. We create a derivative dataset from this of living vs non-living entities test 15 different deep learning models with 5 different architectures and compare to a SOTA linear model. We show that the linear model is not able to solve the decoding task, while almost all the deep learning models are successful, suggesting that in some cases non-linear models are needed to decode neural representations. We also run a comparative study of the models’ performance on individual object categories, and suggest how artificial neural networks can be used to study brain activity.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Dixen, Laurits","Heinrich, Stefan","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798535"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Benchmark","Deep learning","EEG","Neural decoding","Things"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exploring deep learning models for eeg neural decoding","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105000980847","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798535","identifier":[{"id":"105000980847","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-65008d59-2725-4f72-af0d-65781c75a91a"],"keyword":["Benchmark","Deep Learning","EEG","Neural Decoding","THINGS"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-542395422C230DF5EB64C8848F0D378D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, brAIn lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Laurits","key":"dixenpau","last":"Dixen","locid":"e20164d2-7e0c-4f05-88be-ca477dfc5f8c","name":"Dixen, Laurits","orcid":"0009-0001-4447-5222","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefan","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","locid":"1abda59f-c493-4824-a7b1-7470d66f08d1","name":"Heinrich, Stefan","orcid":"0000-0001-9913-3206","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-82487-6_12","isbn":["9783031824869"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"162-175","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"International Symposium on Artificial Intelligence and Neuroscience"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105000980847"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-65008d59-2725-4f72-af0d-65781c75a91a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Exploring Deep Learning Models for EEG Neural Decoding","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798535","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Difficulty is one of the key drivers of player engagement and it is often one of the aspects that designers tweak most to optimise the player experience; operationalising it is, therefore, a crucial task for game development studios. A common practice consists of creating metrics out of data collected by player interactions with the content; however, this allows for estimation only after the content is released and does not consider the characteristics of potential future players. In this article, we present a number of potential solutions for the estimation of difficulty under such conditions, and we showcase the results of a comparative study intended to understand which method and which types of data perform better in different scenarios. The results reveal that models trained on a combination of cohort statistics and simulated data produce the most accurate estimations of difficulty in all scenarios. Furthermore, among these models, artificial neural networks show the most consistent results.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Kristensen, Jeppe Theiss","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-759405"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Artificial neural networks","Cohort Statistics","Difficulty estimation","Game Development","Player Engagement"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-MAE"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-MAE","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"difficulty modelling in mobile puzzle games an empirical study on different methods to combine player analytics and simulated data","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2024/5592373","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-759405","identifier":[{"id":"85198125932","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-67ea5bcd-daf9-4f77-b2f0-5519c55900a1"],"keyword":["Artificial Neural Networks","Cohort Statistics","Difficulty Estimation","Game Development","Player Engagement"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-70700642ED13DCF76AD957F35A74411A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2024/5592373","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102850721/IJCGT_2023_Difficulty_Modelling_in_Free_to_Play_Puzzle_Games.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"a p moller maersk","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-MAE","l3nl":"A.P. Møller – Mærsk","l3nn":"A.P. Moller - Maersk","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"A.P. Moller - Maersk","namevar":["A.P. Møller - Mærsk A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-MAE","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeppe Theiss","key":"kristensenpau","last":"Kristensen","name":"Kristensen, Jeppe Theiss","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["A.P. Møller – Mærsk","All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Private Research","Universities"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["itu-67ea5bcd-daf9-4f77-b2f0-5519c55900a1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Difficulty Modelling in Mobile Puzzle Games: An Empirical Study on Different Methods to Combine Player Analytics and Simulated Data","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-759405","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
