Aalborg University"],"title":"Preface","type":"Book Preface, Encycl. Entry","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-104909","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"In the context of a project assessing the effects of “Arts in Hospitals” the present study aimed at exploring the fruitfulness of integrating psychophysiological methods to ethnographic approaches. We assess whether significant differences can be detected in cognitive processing when attending figurative or abstract art that has been manifestly reported as pleasant or unpleasant by the subject. The experiment measures the absolute power of different EEG frequency bands (i.e. delta, theta, alpha, beta & gamma) in two experimental conditions: i) abstract paintings and ii) figurative paintings. Eye-tracking and self-report data were gathered simultaneously. Each condition included 20 paintings (total of 40), which were pre-selected and assigned to each condition following a rigorous procedure involving an external contributor. A total of 30 non-artist participants (10 females, average age 24.6 (s=4.6)) were recruited for the study. Every test session lasted around 90 minutes (including preparation). Band power analysis for the five investigated frequency bands revealed that largest significant differences between the two conditions were apparent in Theta, Alpha and Beta activations. Based on the results and existing tendencies in the EEG literature, it can be postulated that figurative art viewing involves higher mental engagement, more demanding information retrieval and memory-related cognitive processes than abstract art. Therefore, it could seem to be counter-intuitive the fact that abstract art - with a lesser demand in cognitive load - isgenerally considered to be anxiety-provoking by virtue of its alleged ambiguity (e.g. the emotional congruence theory), independently of its aesthetic effects on particular subjects.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Baceviciute, Sarune","Bruni, Luis Emilio","Burelli, Paolo","Wulff-Jensen, Andreas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-073730"],"event":{"end":"2016-09-01","place":"Vienna, at","start":"2016-08-29","title":"Conference of the International Association of Empirical Aesthetics","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Abstract art","EEG","Eye tracking","Figurative art","Psychophysiology","Self Report"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"differences in cognitive processing when appreciating figurative and abstract art can be detected by integrating eeg and eye tracking data","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.science-of-aesthetics.org/data/proceedings/IAEACongressProceedings2016.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-073730","ids":["aau-5182a6db-e56e-4c57-8081-be5af4204237"],"keyword":["EEG","abstract art","band power","eye-tracking","figurative art","psychophysiology","self-report"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-518C3115A50FC987398907A388175DCE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.science-of-aesthetics.org/data/proceedings/IAEACongressProceedings2016.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sarune","key":"baceviciutepau","last":"Baceviciute","locid":"d8c45dc3-94ff-4e7d-a7a6-aa99df6357c2","name":"Baceviciute, Sarune","orcid":"0000-0003-1867-3361","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luis Emilio","key":"brunipau","last":"Bruni","locid":"c7095325-82f2-4f1a-8e0e-703e01500273","name":"Bruni, Luis Emilio","orcid":"0000-0002-3019-5043","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andreas","key":"wulffjensenpau","last":"Wulff-Jensen","locid":"8ea017e4-4ee0-43b9-81d4-1a337ca2fb4a","name":"Wulff-Jensen, Andreas","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-5182a6db-e56e-4c57-8081-be5af4204237"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Differences in cognitive processing when appreciating figurative and abstract art can be detected by integrating EEG and eye-tracking data","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-073730","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"This chapter about storytelling and interactivity in storytelling first explains on various serious games examples foundations of storytelling. Then storytelling in Interactive Media with regard to serious games is described. Further the current state of the art on Interactive Digital Storytelling is presented including example experiences, authoring tools and challenges in the field combined with examples of serious games. This chapter closes concluding with open storytelling challenges and opportunities in serious games development and recommending further literature on the subject.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Kampa, Antonia","Haake, Susanne","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-123112"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"storytelling in serious games","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-123112","identifier":[{"id":"000389802100019","type":"wos"},{"id":"84992751230","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-fba4e587-cd45-4ebe-9064-893de5270fb2"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-56BCDE14D44E4F5BDA48321EF92DCA52"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Light Research","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Antonia","key":"kampapau","last":"Kampa","name":"Kampa, Antonia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Susanne","key":"haakepau","last":"Haake","name":"Haake, Susanne","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ralf","key":"dörnerpedh","last":"Dörner","name":"Dörner, Ralf","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Stefan","key":"göbelpedh","last":"Göbel","name":"Göbel, Stefan","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Michael","key":"kickmeierrustpedh","last":"Kickmeier-Rust","name":"Kickmeier-Rust, Michael","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Maic","key":"masuchpedh","last":"Masuch","name":"Masuch, Maic","rank":7,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Katharina","key":"zweigpedh","last":"Zweig","name":"Zweig, Katharina","rank":8,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-46152-6_19","isbn":["9783319461526"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"521-539","place":"Cham","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"International GI-Dagstuhl Seminar 15283, Dagstuhl Castle, Germany, July 5-10, 2015, Revised Selected Papers","title":"Entertainment Computing and Serious Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-fba4e587-cd45-4ebe-9064-893de5270fb2"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Storytelling in Serious Games","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-123112","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"This chapter about storytelling and interactivity in storytelling first explains on various serious games examples foundations of storytelling. Then storytelling in Interactive Media with regard to serious games is described. Further the current state of the art on Interactive Digital Storytelling is presented including example experiences, authoring tools and challenges in the field combined with examples of serious games. This chapter closes concluding with open storytelling challenges and opportunities in serious games development and recommending further literature on the subject.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Kampa, Antonia","Haake, Susanne","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381500"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","RheinMain University of Applied Sciences","University of Education Weingarten"],"keyword":["Interactive digital storytelling","Interactive media","Narrating techniques","Serious games"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"storytelling in serious games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-46152-6_19","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381500","identifier":[{"id":"84992751230","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-0fd1b426-e93f-4520-95ac-7b80137b21c0"],"keyword":["Interactive digital storytelling","Interactive media","Narrating techniques","Serious games"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-23356E0713A76FB890CBF28D9A94036E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-46152-6_19","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"rheinmain university of applied sciences","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"RheinMain University of Applied Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"university of education weingarten","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Education Weingarten","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Antonia","key":"kampapau","last":"Kampa","name":"Kampa, Antonia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Susanne","key":"haakepau","last":"Haake","name":"Haake, Susanne","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-46152-6_19","isbn":["9783319461519"],"issn":["00010782"],"pages":"521-539","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Entertainment Computing and Serious Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0fd1b426-e93f-4520-95ac-7b80137b21c0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Storytelling in serious games","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381500","year":"2016","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"The idea of this pilot experiment within the context of the “Art in Hospitals” project was to explore the fruitfulness and future perspectives for integrating psychophysiological methods to the ethnographic approach so far implemented in the project. As a pilot study it serves to open the doors to experimental avenues that can support the ethnographic investigation. Therefore in order to form a basis for applications of these methodologies in future studies in the field, this pilot was concentrated in one of the initial premises of the project, which intended to challenge current recommendations for art in hospitals. Most of these guidelines favor figurative over abstract art, based on ideas leaning to the emotional congruence theory, which would claim that abstract art leads to ambiguity and therefore it could augment the current emotional base-line of an already stressed patient. The early ethnographic studies of the “Art in Hospitals” project challenged this perspective by investigating the positive or negative effects of “lower-level” specific features (e.g.: bright colors vs. darker, contrast, predominant shapes) independent of whether they were present in abstract or figurative art, which as such could not be said to have universal positive or negative effects respectively. In this sense it was retained necessary to assess whether significant differences can be detected in cognitive processes when processing figurative or abstract art that has been manifestly reported as pleasant or unpleasant by the subject. The experiment measures the absolute power of different EEG frequency bands (i.e. delta, theta, alpha, beta & gamma) in two experimental conditions: i) abstract paintings and ii) figurative paintings. Eye-tracking and self-report data were gathered simultaneously. Each condition included 20 paintings (total of 40), which were pre-selected and assigned to each condition following a rigorous procedure involving an external contributor.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Baceviciute, Sarune","Bruni, Luis Emilio","Burelli, Paolo","Wulff-Jensen, Andreas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-077529"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"art in hospitals project psychophysiology experiment","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/243948934/Art_in_Hospitals_Psychophysiology_Report.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-077529","ids":["aau-5e9a47e0-88c1-4129-917e-54b68a8ba261"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-AEEA9D8B4BF7286036F7733D063434AE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/243948934/Art_in_Hospitals_Psychophysiology_Report.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sarune","key":"baceviciutepau","last":"Baceviciute","locid":"d8c45dc3-94ff-4e7d-a7a6-aa99df6357c2","name":"Baceviciute, Sarune","orcid":"0000-0003-1867-3361","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luis Emilio","key":"brunipau","last":"Bruni","locid":"c7095325-82f2-4f1a-8e0e-703e01500273","name":"Bruni, Luis Emilio","orcid":"0000-0002-3019-5043","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andreas","key":"wulffjensenpau","last":"Wulff-Jensen","locid":"8ea017e4-4ee0-43b9-81d4-1a337ca2fb4a","name":"Wulff-Jensen, Andreas","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"12","publisher":"Journal series: Arkitektur & Design  AD Files)","publisher_org":"Journal series: Arkitektur & Design (A&D Files)","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-5e9a47e0-88c1-4129-917e-54b68a8ba261"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Art in Hospitals Project : Psychophysiology experiment","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-077529","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Building on the definition of cinematography (Soanes and Stevenson, Oxford dictionary of English. Oxford University Press, Oxford/New York, 2005), game cinematography can be defined as the art of visualizing the content of a computer game. The relationship between game cinematography and its traditional counterpart is extremely tight as, in both cases, the aim of cinematography is to control the viewer’s perspective and affect his or her perception of the events represented. However, game events are not necessarily pre-scripted and player interaction has a major role on the quality of a game experience; therefore, the role of the camera and the challenges connected to it are different in game cinematography as the virtual camera has to both dynamically react to unexpected events to correctly convey the game story and take into consideration player actions and desires to support her interaction with the virtual world. This chapter provides an overview of the evolution of the research in virtual and game cinematography, ranging from its early focus on how to control and animate the virtual camera to support interaction to its relationship with player experience and emotions. Furthermore, we will show and discuss a number of emerging research directions.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S02-090.62-004260"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Cinematography Evolution","Game Cinematography","Player Interaction","Viewer Perspective","Virtual camera control"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"game cinematography from camera control to player emotions","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S02-090.62-004260","ids":["aau-57370cfe-f776-4198-940e-9ba331446720","itu-acf3c366-b37d-4e6b-af2f-015594fa1a64"],"keyword":["Cinematography Evolution","Game Cinematography","Player Interaction","Viewer Perspective","Virtual Camera Control"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0C5F60C4FD83D7DC09D4AD625193E92C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Light Research","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kostas","key":"karpouzispedh","last":"Karpouzis","name":"Karpouzis, Kostas","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispedh","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-41316-7_11","eisbn":["9783319413167"],"isbn":["9783319413167"],"issn":["25095706"],"pages":"181-195","place":"Cham","publisher":"Springer","series":"Socio-Affektive Computing","status":"p","subtitle":"Theory and Praxis","title":"Emotion in Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-57370cfe-f776-4198-940e-9ba331446720","itu-acf3c366-b37d-4e6b-af2f-015594fa1a64"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Game Cinematography : From Camera Control to Player Emotions","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-090.62-004260","year":"2016","yearsub":2017}
{"abstract":"Research in virtual camera control has focused primarily on finding methods to allow designers to place cameras effectively and efficiently in dynamic and unpredictable environments, and to generate complex and dynamic plans for cinematography in virtual environments. In this article, we propose a novel approach to virtual camera control, which builds upon camera control and player modelling to provide the user with an adaptive point-of-view. To achieve this goal, we propose a methodology to model the player’s preferences on virtual camera movements and we employ the resulting models to tailor the viewpoint movements to the player type and her game-play style. Ultimately, the methodology is applied to a 3D platform game and is evaluated through a controlled experiment; the results suggest that the resulting adaptive cinematographic experience is favoured by some player types and it can generate a positive impact on the game performance.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo","Yannakakis, Georgios N."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-059043"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU"],"country":["Malta"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Malta"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"adapting virtual camera behaviour through player modelling","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-059043","identifier":[{"id":"84939981491","type":"scopus"},{"id":"WOS:000352779000002","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-1ec9c65d-d156-4c90-a824-214bdd2ee183"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E3057F2E43BADE4404071450766ACA02"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Light Research","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"mt","key":"university of malta","loc":["Malta","Europe, EU"],"name":"University of Malta","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispau","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s11257-015-9156-4","issn":["09241868","15731391"],"issn_org":["09241868"],"issue":"2","journal":"User Modeling and User-Adapted Interaction","pages":"155-183","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"User Modeling and User-Adapted Interaction","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84939981491"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-1ec9c65d-d156-4c90-a824-214bdd2ee183"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Adapting virtual camera behaviour through player modelling","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-059043","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"In this paper we analyze narrative in interactive media with a special focus on emergent narrative. We detail the criteria for creation of an online questionnaire for the evaluation of emergent narrative as a subjective experience and present the result of a large scale survey in which it was applied. The survey was conducted during a threeweek- period in May-June 2015, receiving answers from 14,259 people. Each participant answered based on one of the twenty games chosen for the investigation. The twenty games were chosen to meet one of three inclusion criteria’s. Firstly on the usage of descriptive narrative in their communities, which indicates a strong emergent narrative experience, secondly their emergent narrative potential and thirdly for their potential to be used as comparative or baseline measurements. The results strongly indicate that the Emergent Narrative questionnaire is effective for measuring the occurrence of an emergent narrative, when compared to the test-subjects’ self-reported experience.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Hannesson, Hakon Jarl","Reimann-Andersen, Thorbjørn","Burelli, Paolo","Bruni, Luis Emilio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-065579"],"event":{"acronym":"ICIDS","end":"2015-12-04","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2015-11-30","title":"8th International Conference on Interactive Digital Storytelling, ICIDS 2015","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"connecting the dots quantifying the narrative experience in interactive media","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-065579","identifier":[{"id":"84951871469","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-3575054c-f9c4-4f92-8945-6d0b98677295"],"l