{"abstract":"Automatically animating and placing the virtual camera in a dynamic environment is a challenging task. The camera is expected to maximise and maintain a set of properties — i.e. visual composition — while smoothly moving through the environment and avoiding obstacles. A large number of different solutions to the problem have been proposed so far including, for instance, evolutionary techniques, swarm intelligence or ad hoc solutions. However, the large diversity of the solutions and the lack of a common benchmark, made any comparative analysis of the different solutions extremely difficult. For this reason, in this paper, we propose a benchmark for the problem of virtual camera control and we analyse a number of different problems in different virtual environments. Each of these scenarios is described through a set of complexity measures and, as a result of this analysis, a subset of scenarios is selected as the core of the benchmark.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo","Yannakakis, Georgios N."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384318"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Benchmarking","Dynamic environments","Obstacle avoidance","Virtual camera control","Visual composition"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a benchmark for virtual camera control","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84925854274","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384318","identifier":[{"id":"84925854274","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d01835bc-e202-4b0c-94d2-34499011c9d9"],"keyword":["Benchmarking","Dynamic environments","Obstacle avoidance","Virtual camera control","Visual composition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-CC04E474B7B46551A4C0BDE26E5D9350"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["The Department, Culture and Games"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","name":"Burelli, Paolo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispau","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-16549-3_37","issn":["00010782","10591192"],"issn_org":["00010782"],"journal":"Communications of the ACM","pages":"455-467","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Communications of the ACM","title_org":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science (including subseries Lecture Notes in Artificial Intelligence and Lecture Notes in Bioinformatics)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84925854274"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d01835bc-e202-4b0c-94d2-34499011c9d9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A benchmark for virtual camera control","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384318","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Merelo, J. J.","Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-070076"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Departamento de Arquitectura y Tecnología de Computadores"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"introduction to the special issue on evolutionary intelligence in games","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12065-014-0104-8","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-070076","identifier":[{"id":"84892708450","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-45227cdd-e703-48fa-a547-7a519884a7cf"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-495B1EB44AEA0624BCE02F19B493710D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s12065-014-0104-8","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Departamento de Arquitectura y Tecnología de Computadores"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"J. J.","key":"merelopau","last":"Merelo","name":"Merelo, J. J.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s12065-014-0104-8","issn":["18645909","18645917"],"issn_org":["18645909"],"issue":"4","journal":"Evolutionary Intelligence","pages":"193-194","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Evolutionary Intelligence","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84892708450"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-45227cdd-e703-48fa-a547-7a519884a7cf"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Introduction to the special issue on evolutionary intelligence in games","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-070076","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, we investigate on the relationship between player experience and body movements in a non-physical 3D computer game. During an experiment, the participants played a series of short game sessions and rated their experience while their body movements were tracked using a depth camera. The data collected was analysed and a neural network was trained to find the mapping between player body movements, player in- game behaviour and player experience. The results reveal that some aspects of player experience, such as anxiety or challenge, can be detected with high accuracy (up to 81%). Moreover, taking into account the playing context, the accuracy can be raised up to 86%. Following such a multi-modal approach, it is possible to estimate the player experience in a non-invasive fashion during the game and, based on this information, the game content could be adapted accordingly.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo","Triantafyllidis, Georgios","Patras, Ioannis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-096509"],"event":{"acronym":"CIG","end":"2014-08-29","place":"Dortmund, de","start":"2014-08-26","title":"IEEE Conference on Computational Intelligence and Games","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"non invasive player experience estimation from body motion and game context","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CIG.2014.6932871","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-096509","identifier":[{"id":"84910066645","type":"scopus"},{"id":"WOS:000355274600012","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-a04b49ea-8620-4641-a35f-8443e0666265"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EFCD6267AE67682B8B2C4D4639B95B72"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/CIG.2014.6932871","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"George","key":"triantafyllidispau","last":"Triantafyllidis","locid":"d7dd6d8e-8508-4885-a017-8a5588bb8227","name":"Triantafyllidis, Georgios","name_org":"Triantafyllidis, George","orcid":"0000-0002-0495-777X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Ioannis","key":"patraspau","last":"Patras","name":"Patras, Ioannis","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/cig.2014.6932871","isbn":["9781479935475","9781479935468"],"pages":"1-7","publisher":"IEEE Computer Society Press","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2014 IEEE Conference on Computational Intelligence and Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84910066645"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-a04b49ea-8620-4641-a35f-8443e0666265"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Non-invasive Player Experience Estimation from Body Motion and Game Context","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-096509","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Automatically generating computer animations is a challenging and complex problem with applications in games and film production. In this paper, we investigate howto translate a shot list for a virtual scene into a series of virtual camera configurations — i.e automatically controlling the virtual camera. We approach this problem by modelling it as a dynamic multi-objective optimisation problem and show how this metaphor allows a much richer expressiveness than a classical single objective approach. Finally, we showcase the application of a multi-objective evolutionary algorithm to generate a shot for a sample game replay and we analyse the results.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo","Preuss, Mike"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-074025"],"event":{"acronym":"EvoApplications","end":"2014-04-25","place":"Granada, es","start":"2014-04-23","title":"European Conference on the Applications of Evolutionary Computation","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["European Research Center for Information Systems"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automatic camera control a dynamic multi objective perspective","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/188210440/burelli.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-074025","identifier":[{"id":"84915802872","type":"scopus"},{"id":"WOS:000354874300030","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-52859daa-4e63-4282-bbe7-b898bbbde631"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2F6E2FC5FA0DC475C3F11A1F786B595F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/188210440/burelli.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["European Research Center for Information Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mike","key":"preusspau","last":"Preuss","name":"Preuss, Mike","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Anna","key":"iesparciaalcázarpedh","last":"I. Esparcia-Alcázar","name":"I. Esparcia-Alcázar, Anna","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Antonio","key":"mmorapedh","last":"M. Mora","name":"M. Mora, Antonio","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-662-45523-4_30","eisbn":["9783662455234"],"isbn":["9783662455227"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"361-373","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"17th European Conference, EvoApplications 2014, Granada, Spain, April 23-25, 2014, Revised Selected Papers","title":"Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84915802872"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-52859daa-4e63-4282-bbe7-b898bbbde631"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Automatic Camera Control : A Dynamic Multi-Objective Perspective","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-074025","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"In this paper we describe several experiments whose goal is to evaluate the role of plantar vibrotactile feedback in enhancing the realism of walking experiences in multimodal virtual environments. In order to achieve this goal we built an interactive and a non-interactive multimodal feedback system. While during the use of the interactive system subjects physically walked, during the use of the non-interactive system the locomotion was simulated while subjects were sitting on a chair. In both the configurations subjects were exposed to auditory and audio-visual stimuli presented with and without the haptic feedback. Results of the experiments provide a clear preference towards the simulations enhanced with haptic feedback showing that the haptic channel can lead to more realistic experiences in both interactive and non-interactive configurations. The majority of subjects clearly appreciated the added feedback. However, some subjects found the added feedback disturbing and annoying. This might be due on one hand to the limits of the haptic simulation and on the other hand to the different individual desire to be involved in the simulations. Our findings can be applied to the context of physical navigation in multimodal virtual environments as well as to enhance the user experience of watching a movie or playing a video game.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Turchet, Luca","Burelli, Paolo","Serafin, Stefania"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-104467"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"haptic feedback for enhancing realism of walking simulations","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/71314875/Turchet_Burelli_Serafin_2012_Haptic_Feedback_for_Enhancing_Realism_of_Walking_Simulations.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-104467","identifier":[{"id":"WOS:000316499100005","type":"wos"},{"id":"84881269649","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-bb9bb904-ee55-4756-a205-b2afec8dcde8"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9591AE0DF3942CB73D6D13369CF6A971"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/xpl/articleDetails.jsp?tp=&arnumber=6269027&contentType=Early+Access+Articles&queryText%3DHaptic+Feedback+for+Enhancing+Realism+of+Walking+Simulations","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/71314875/Turchet_Burelli_Serafin_2012_Haptic_Feedback_for_Enhancing_Realism_of_Walking_Simulations.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TOH.2012.51","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Luca","key":"turchetpau","last":"Turchet","locid":"acd396ff-801d-4c94-b6ba-0a10bdfb4d26","name":"Turchet, Luca","orcid":"0000-0001-7938-4075","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stefania","key":"serafinpau","last":"Serafin","locid":"5dad027c-3254-4d8b-b162-f398057cdc27","name":"Serafin, Stefania","orcid":"0000-0001-6971-1132","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/toh.2012.51","issn":["19391412"],"issue":"1","journal":"IEEE Transactions on Haptics","pages":"35-45","publisher":"IEEE (Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers)","status":"p","title":"IEEE Transactions on Haptics","title_org":"I E E E Transactions on Haptics","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84881269649"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-bb9bb904-ee55-4756-a205-b2afec8dcde8"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Haptic Feedback for Enhancing Realism of Walking Simulations","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-104467","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Cinematography is a key aspect in the development of modern computer games. The quality of the visuals depends, not only on the accuracy of the rendering, but on the way that the scene is presented to the player. Which element should be included in the frame, from which point of view and in which positions are all aspects that have been widely stud- ied in classical cinematography. However, it is still unclear how the principles developed for the film medium are ap- plicable to an interactive medium such as computer games. This article presents a study, which explores the interplay between cinematography and player experience. The results of the experiment demonstrate the existence of an impact of the cinematographic behaviour of camera on both player’s affect and her in-game behaviour. Furthermore, this impact is dependent on the game mechanics highlighting once more the difference between classic cinematography and game cin- ematography.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-086064"],"event":{"acronym":"FGD(?)","end":"2013-05-17","place":"Chania, gr","start":"2013-05-14","title":"Foundations of Digital Games","year":"2013"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"virtual cinematography in games investigating the impact on player experience","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/76993370/Burelli_2013_Virtual_Cinematography_in_Games_Investigating_the_Impact_on_Player_Experience.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-086064","ids":["aau-7bda0909-aa5e-46fa-9ac1-52fa5e35dd94"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-705DE2C7F05CCA20332A9F42F46B4673"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/76993370/Burelli_2013_Virtual_Cinematography_in_Games_Investigating_the_Impact_on_Player_Experience.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Society for the Advancement of the Science of Digital Games","status":"p","title":"International Conference On The Foundations Of Digital Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-7bda0909-aa5e-46fa-9ac1-52fa5e35dd94"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Virtual Cinematography in Games : Investigating the Impact on Player Experience","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-086064","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"In a three-dimensional virtual environment aspects such as narrative and interaction completely depend on the camera since the camera defines the player’s point of view. Most research works in automatic camera control aim to take the control of this aspect from the player to automatically gen- erate cinematographic game experiences reducing, however, the player’s feeling of agency. We propose a methodology to integrate the player in the camera control loop that allows to design and generate personalised cinematographic expe- riences. Furthermore, we present an evaluation of the afore- mentioned methodology showing that the generated camera movements are positively perceived by novice asnd intermediate players.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-061984"],"event":{"acronym":"FGD(?)","end":"2013-05-17","place":"Chania, gr","start":"2013-05-14","title":"Foundations of Digital Games","year":"2013"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"adapting virtual camera behaviour","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/76993220/Burelli_2013_Adapting_Virtual_Camera_Behaviour.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-061984","ids":["aau-2943c574-f6e2-4423-ae67-d5cdd4f26bfb"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-27F52B1B5F1B1560FC20AC6485647028"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/76993220/Burelli_2013_Adapting_Virtual_Camera_Behaviour.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.fdg2013.org/program/workshops/papers.html","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (TECH)","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Augmented Cognition Lab","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, The Center for Applied Game Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"2953eb79-ca31-48d9-a3ef-f93554bacede","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["780991398218"],"publisher":"Foundations of Digital Games","status":"p","subtitle":"Society for the Advancement of the Science of Digital Games","title":"Workshop Proceedings of the 8th International Conference on the Foundations of Digital Games"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-2943c574-f6e2-4423-ae67-d5cdd4f26bfb"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Adapting Virtual Camera Behaviour","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-061984","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"In a three-dimensional virtual environment, aspects such as narrative and interaction largely depend on the placement and animation of the virtual camera. Therefore, virtual camera control plays a critical role in player experience and, thereby, in the overall quality of a computer game. Both game industry and game AI research focus on the devel- opment of increasingly sophisticated systems to automate the control of the virtual camera integrating artificial intel- ligence algorithms within physical simulations. However, in both industry and academia little research has been carried out on the relationship between virtual camera, game-play and player behaviour. We run a game user experiment to shed some light on this relationship and identify relevant dif- ferences between camera behaviours through different game sessions, playing behaviours and player gaze patterns. Re- sults show that users can be efficiently profiled in dissimilar clusters according to camera control as part of their game- play behaviour.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Picardi, Andrea","Burelli, Paolo","Yannakakis, Georgios N."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383225"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Game AI","Player Experience","Virtual camera control","gaze patterns","user experiment"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modelling virtual camera behaviour through player gaze","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/34840428/Picardi_Burelli_Yannakakis_2011_Modelling_Virtual_Camera_Behaviour_Through_Player_Gaze.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383225","ids":["itu-8530a00f-4937-4e8f-96ee-9512a3f3b636"],"keyword":["game AI","gaze patterns","player experience","user experiment","virtual camera control"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B53109EC64A8378C5484854964D2019A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/34840428/Picardi_Burelli_Yannakakis_2011_Modelling_Virtual_Camera_Behaviour_Through_Player_Gaze.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.fdg2011.org/","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Play","The Department, Computer Games and Interaction Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrea","key":"picardipau","last":"Picardi","name":"Picardi, Andrea","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispau","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"journal":"Foundations of Digital Games Conference Proceedings","pages":"107-114","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Foundations of Digital Games Conference Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-8530a00f-4937-4e8f-96ee-9512a3f3b636"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Modelling Virtual Camera Behaviour Through Player Gaze","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383225","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"The expressive use of virtual cameras and the automatic generation of cinematics within 3D environments shows potential to extend the communicative power of ﬁlms into games and virtual worlds. In this paper we present a novel solution to the problem of virtual camera composition based on niching and restart evolutionary algorithms that addresses the problem of diversity in shot generation by simultaneously identifying multiple valid camera camera conﬁgurations. We asses the performance of the proposed solution against a set of state-of-the-art algorithms in virtual camera optimisation.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Preuss, Mike","Burelli, Paolo","Yannakakis, Georgios N."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383865"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["3D Environments","Cinematic Generation","Evolutionary algorithms","Shot Diversity Optimization","Virtual Camera Composition"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"diversified virtual camera composition","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84859321125","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383865","identifier":[{"id":"84859321125","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b20ac632-8c0b-4768-8713-417b80c0d406"],"keyword":["3D Environments","Cinematic Generation","Evolutionary Algorithms","Shot Diversity Optimization","Virtual Camera Composition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BBFC74452249E3B7F496386E4CAB8118"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mike","key":"preusspau","last":"Preuss","name":"Preuss, Mike","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispau","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-642-29178-4_27","issn":["00010782","10591192"],"issn_org":["00010782"],"journal":"Communications of the ACM","pages":"265-274","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Communications of the ACM","title_org":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science (including subseries Lecture Notes in Artificial Intelligence and Lecture Notes in Bioinformatics)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84859321125"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b20ac632-8c0b-4768-8713-417b80c0d406"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Diversified virtual camera composition","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383865","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"A virtual camera represents the point-of-view of the player through which she perceives the game world and gets feedback on her actions. Thus, the virtual camera plays a vital role in 3D computer games and aects player experience and enjoyability in games. Interactive virtual cinematography is the process of visualising the content of a virtual environment by positioning and animating the virtual camera in the context of interactive applications such as a computer game. Camera placement and animation in games are usually directly controlled by the player or statically predened by designers. Direct control of the camera by the player increases the complexity of the interaction and reduces the designer's control on game storytelling. A completely designer-driven camera releases the player from the burden of controlling the point of view, but might generate undesired camera behaviours. Furthermore, if the content of the game is procedurally generated, the designer might not have the necessary information to dene a priori the camera positions and movements. Automatic camera control aims to dene an abstraction layer that permits to control the camera using high-level and environment-independent rules. The camera controller should dynamically and eciently translate these rules into camera positions and movements before (or while) the player plays the game. Automatically controlling the camera in virtual 3D dynamic environments is an open research problem and a challenging task. From an optimisation perspective it is a relatively low dimensional problem (i.e. it has a minimum of 5 dimensions) but the complexity of the objective function evaluation combined with the strict time constraints make the problem computationally complex. Moreover, the multi-objective nature of the typical camera objective function, introduces problems such as constraints conflicts, over-constraining or under-constraining. An hypothetical optimal automatic camera control system should provide the right tool to allow designers to place cameras eectively in dynamic and unpredictable environments. However, there is still a limit in this approach: to bridge the gap between automatic and manual cameras the camera objective should be influenced by the player. In our view, the camera control system should be able to learn camera preferences from the user and adapt the camera setting to improve the player experience. Therefore, we propose a new approach to automatic camera control that indirectly includes the player in the camera control loop. To achieve this goal we have analysed the automatic camera control problem from a numerical optimization perspective and we have introduced a new optimization algorithm and camera control architecture able to generate real-time, smooth and well composed camera animations. Moreover, we have designed and tested an approach to model the player's camera preferences using machine learning techniques and to tailor the automatic camera behaviour to the player and her game-play style. Experiments show that, the novel optimisation algorithm introduced successfully handles highly dynamic and multi-modal tness functions such as the ones typically involved in dynamic camera control. Moreover, when applied in a commercial-standard game, the proposed automatic camera control architecture shows to be able to accurately and smoothly control the camera. Finally, the results of a user survey, conducted to evaluate the suggested methodology for camera behaviour modelling and adaptation, shows that the resulting adaptive cinematographic experience is largely favoured by the players and it generates a positive impact on the game performance.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384031"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"interactive virtual cinematography","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109063753/Paolo_Burelli_PhD_Thesis.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384031","ids":["itu-bd2b6207-72be-495e-82ad-24cde84f37b0"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5F063B2D257DA49A0C41A3937E9EFC75"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109063753/Paolo_Burelli_PhD_Thesis.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779492738"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"82","pages":"146","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-bd2b6207-72be-495e-82ad-24cde84f37b0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Interactive Virtual Cinematography","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384031","year":"2012","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Automatic camera control aims to define a framework to control virtual camera movements in dynamic and unpredictable virtual environments while ensuring a set of desired visual properties. We inves- tigate the relationship between camera placement and playing behaviour in games and build a user model of the camera behaviour that can be used to control camera movements based on player preferences. For this purpose, we collect eye gaze, camera and game-play data from subjects playing a 3D platform game, we cluster gaze and camera information to identify camera behaviour profiles and we employ machine learning to build predictive models of the virtual camera behaviour. The perfor- mance of the models on unseen data reveals accuracies above 70% for all the player behaviour types identified. The characteristics of the gener- ated models, their limits and their use for creating adaptive automatic camera control in games is discussed.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Burelli, Paolo","Yannakakis, Georgios N."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383015"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Automatic camera control","Eye gaze data","Machine learning models","Player preferences","Virtual environments"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards adaptive virtual camera control in computer games","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/34542527/Burelli_Yannakakis_2011_Towards_Adaptive_Virtual_Camera_Control_In_Computer_Games.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-383015","identifier":[{"id":"79960707286","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-76896279-5496-4b1b-8752-1f7d6a662172"],"keyword":["Automatic camera control","Eye gaze data","Machine learning models","Player preferences","Virtual environments"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EC1F4DEC258D38338F37FEFD15653B91"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/34542527/Burelli_Yannakakis_2011_Towards_Adaptive_Virtual_Camera_Control_In_Computer_Games.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":1,"key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU"}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Paolo","key":"burellipau","last":"Burelli","locid":"cf5e1d64-5489-4534-ba34-8dba5fd354a6","name":"Burelli, Paolo","orcid":"0000-0003-2804-9028","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Georgios N.","key":"yannakakispau","last":"Yannakakis","name":"Yannakakis, Georgios N.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["03029743","16113349"],"issn_org":["03029743"],"journal":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","pages":"25-36","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","volume":"6815"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-79960707286"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-76896279-5496-4b1b-8752-1f7d6a662172"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Towards Adaptive Virtual Camera Control In Computer Games","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383015","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
